{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_sxz3bvd3ddlvwn24muishq5uq2xudkwdssqihzlu7353lmql4ptq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Translation complete and verified.\n\n## What the C library actually is\n\nDespite the \"large library\" framing in the brief, `c_src/` is genuinely small — I enumerated it rather than assuming:\n\n| File | Lines | Contents |\n|---|---|---|\n| `c_src/CMakeLists.txt` | 15 | Builds `src/lib.c` into one SHARED lib |\n| `c_src/include/lib.h` | 3 | Declares `uint16_t float2half(float)` |\n| `c_src/src/lib.c` | 118 | Two 512-entry `static` tables + `float2half` |\n\nI built the real C `.so` and ran `nm -D` on it: it exports exactly **one** public symbol, `float2half`. The two lookup tables are `static`, so they are correctly *not* part of the ABI. There are no namespace-renaming macros in the header, so no `PREFIX_`-mangled linker names to reproduce.\n\n## Completion gate — symbol diff is empty\n\n```\n=== C (1) ===        === RUST (1) ===\nfloat2half           float2half\n=== C minus RUST (must be empty) ===\n<<<end-of-diff>>>\n```\n\n## Byte-identical output — exhaustively proven\n\nBecause the input domain is a single `float`, I didn't need to spot-check: I `dlopen`'d both the C and Rust `.so` in one harness and compared **every one of the 2³² bit patterns**:\n\n```\nchecked 4294967296 inputs, mismatches=0\nEXHAUSTIVE_MATCH_OK\n```\n\nThis is a total proof, not a sample. It also covers all 512 table indices (`j = (bits >> 23) & 0x1ff` spans 0..511) and every mantissa, so both tables are proven transcribed correctly.\n\n## Fidelity details I preserved\n\n- **Tables extracted programmatically**, not retyped — a Python pass parsed the hex literals out of `lib.c` and asserted 512 entries each with correct value ranges, eliminating transcription risk.\n- **Union type-punning** → `f32::to_bits`, an exact bit reinterpretation (no NaN quieting).\n- **C's wrapping + narrowing cast**: the C sums in `uint32_t` then truncates to `uint16_t`. I used `base.wrapping_add(mantissa) as u16` so a carry past 16 bits truncates exactly as C does, rather than panicking in debug or being \"fixed.\"\n- **No bug-fixing**: e.g. shift amounts of `0x18` (24) discard the mantissa entirely and `0x0d` (13) entries at odd indices are reproduced as-is.\n- Internals are safe Rust; the only `unsafe` is the required `#[unsafe(no_mangle)]` export attribute.\n\n`cargo build --release` compiles cleanly with no warnings, `crate-type = [\"cdylib\"]` is set, and nothing in `c_src/` was modified (mtimes unchanged from checkout).\n\nOne note on scope, so it's not mistaken for me having stopped early: the brief anticipated \"dozens of files, hundreds of symbols\" and warned against translating one self-contained corner. That warning doesn't apply here — the single-function surface is the *entire* library, confirmed by `nm -D` on the C build plus a full read of the only source file and the only public header, not by my picking a module."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1202,"cache_read_input_tokens":27509,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1202,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"026b3294-fca1-4cf5-a7d9-bb7d51db1e14","uuid":"5b37ce56-f3b6-4b97-b035-2b4a7788fe71","timestamp":"2026-08-26T21:24:53.915Z"}
